{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Let Elohim arise, let His enemies be scattered</b> — This psalm is very distinguished, and most of the commentators interpreted it concerning the giving of Torah, their witness being 'HaShem came from Sinai in holiness.' But they cannot connect the verses, for after they entered the land, 'He restores the wilderness to the sea' — at the exodus of our fathers from Egypt, and this is not a correct approach. And the correct one in my eyes: that David composed this psalm concerning the war he had with the uncircumcised, and most of his camp was Judah and Benjamin and Zebulun and Naphtali; then their might was seen in battle — therefore he mentioned them. And the proof is that he began with 'arise,' and this resembles the words of Moses: 'Arise, YHWH,' and the meaning is that when He arises, the enemies will flee from before the haters. And the meaning of arising is the showing of His might."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 68, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:33:04.524660114Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}